<Guide
  title="Epley Maneuver Angles: 45°, the Head-Hang, 90° and Roll"
  description="The four head positions of the Epley maneuver in degrees, why the angles are hard to judge on yourself, and how to check them with your phone."
  path="/guides/epley-maneuver-angles/"
  faq={faq}
  sources={sources}
>
  <p class="answer">
    The standard Epley maneuver uses four head positions: turned <strong>45°</strong> toward the
    affected ear while sitting, then lying back with the head still turned and tipped back below the
    body, then turned <strong>90°</strong> to the other side, then rolled with the body so the face
    points down toward the floor. Each is held for at least 30 seconds.<cite><a href="#src-1">[1]</a></cite>
    The hard part at home is not remembering the sequence — it's knowing whether you actually reached
    those angles.
  </p>

  <h2>The four positions, in degrees</h2>
  <p>
    This is the geometry as clinical references describe it, for the posterior-canal form of BPPV. If
    your clinician taught you a variation, theirs is the one to follow.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Position</th><th>Head angle</th><th>Hold</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>1. Sitting</td>
        <td>Turned 45° toward the affected ear<cite><a href="#src-1">[1]</a></cite></td>
        <td>Brief — this is the setup</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>2. Lying back</td>
        <td>Still turned 45°; neck extended so the head is below the body, commonly given as about 30° <mark>[VERIFY]</mark></td>
        <td>≥ 30 s<cite><a href="#src-1">[1]</a></cite></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>3. Turned across</td>
        <td>Rotated 90° to the other side, keeping the head tipped back<cite><a href="#src-1">[1]</a></cite></td>
        <td>≥ 30 s</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>4. Rolled</td>
        <td>Body rolls onto that side; head keeps turning so it faces about 135° from straight up — nose angled toward the floor<cite><a href="#src-1">[1]</a></cite></td>
        <td>≥ 30 s</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Then you sit up slowly. The angles add up: 45° in position 1, 90° more in position 3, and another
    roughly 90° as you roll in position 4. Each step builds on the one before, so an error in the
    first turn carries through every later position.
  </p>

  <h2>Why 45° is harder to hit than it sounds</h2>
  <p>
    The maneuver works by moving loose crystals (otoconia) through one of the inner ear's canals and back out
    into the part of the ear where they don't cause vertigo. The 45° turn lines the posterior canal up with the
    direction of the lying-back movement. Turn too little and the movement isn't lined up with the canal,
    so the crystals don't travel the way they need to.
  </p>
  <p>Three things make the angle hard to judge on yourself:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>You can't see your own head.</strong> A video shows someone else's angle. Your only reference is how
      it feels, and people rarely know how far they've actually turned their neck.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dizziness makes people stop short.</strong> Positions 2 and 3 are meant to bring on
      spinning in an affected ear. The natural response is to ease off the angle that's making you
      dizzy — and that angle is the one the maneuver needs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Turning and tipping interfere.</strong> Lying back tends to straighten the head out of
      the 45° turn. Rolling in position 4 tends to leave the head behind the shoulders. Each is one
      axis quietly drifting while you concentrate on the other.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Checking the angles with a phone</h2>
  <p>
    A phone held flat against your forehead moves exactly as your head moves, and its motion sensors
    report orientation continuously. BPPVCoach uses that to answer one question during each position:
    <em>is your head where the reference position says it should be?</em>
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p style="margin-top:0">
      You set a neutral reference first — sitting upright, facing forward. The phone then tracks two
      axes: how far your head is tipped back or forward, and how far it's tilted toward one ear. Once
      you're lying down, the 45° and 90° turns show up on that second axis. Each lying position shows
      green when both axes are inside a set tolerance (±12° for positions 2 and 3, a little wider for
      the roll), and a 30-second timer runs while you hold it. The sitting turn itself isn't measured.
      It doesn't diagnose anything, choose the ear, or decide whether you should do the maneuver — it
      only measures the angles.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/session/">Start a guided session</a> · <a href="/how-it-works/">How the measurement works</a></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Examples</h2>
  <h3>The turn that felt like 45°</h3>
  <p>
    A typical case: someone turns toward their affected ear until it feels like a proper turn and
    lies back. In position 2 the indicator stays yellow — the head has rolled back toward
    face-up, so the turn is well short of the target. They turn further, the indicator goes green,
    and they hold it there instead of holding the shallower angle that felt right. <em>(This is an
    illustration of the pattern, not a measured result.)</em>
  </p>
  <h3>Losing the tilt in position 3</h3>
  <p>
    Turning the head 90° while lying flat is awkward, and the easy way to do it is to lift the chin
    back up to level. The rotation is then right but the head is no longer tipped back. A two-axis
    reading shows the rotation in range and the tilt out of range, which a mirror or video wouldn't.
  </p>
  <h3>When the angles were right and it still didn't work</h3>
  <p>
    Getting every position green doesn't guarantee a result. The maneuver treats posterior-canal
    BPPV. Other canals need different maneuvers, and other causes of dizziness aren't helped by
    repositioning at all. If you've done it carefully and nothing changes, the next step is a
    clinician, not more repetitions — see <a href="/guides/did-the-epley-maneuver-work/">how to tell
    whether it worked</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you use any angle reference</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Use the side your clinician identified. Guessing it from which side feels dizzier isn't reliable.</li>
    <li>Have someone with you if you can, and a bed or surface where you can lie back safely.</li>
    <li>Neck problems (cervical stenosis, limited neck mobility) are a reason to check with a clinician first.<cite><a href="#src-1">[1]</a></cite></li>
    <li>Nausea is the most common side effect.<cite><a href="#src-1">[1]</a></cite> Sit up slowly at the end.</li>
    <li>The US clinical guideline recommends against restricting your posture after the maneuver (such as sleeping upright).<cite><a href="#src-2">[2]</a></cite></li>
  </ul>
</Guide>
